
*.pyc

test.py

# raw_data is regenerable by the scrapers and far too large to track (the CafeF PDF archive
# alone is ~1.7 GB per ticker) — with ONE exception, opened up at the bottom of this file:
# raw_data/cafef/financials/. Git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded,
# so the path is opened one level at a time rather than with a bare `raw_data/`.
raw_data/*
!raw_data/cafef/
raw_data/cafef/*
!raw_data/cafef/financials/

# the DeepDoc DB detector weights for the onnx OCR engine (~4.7 MB). Fetched on first use from
# HuggingFace (InfiniFlow/deepdoc) by onnx_ocr.ensure_det_model; the vendored *.py detection code
# in src/web_scraper/_deepdoc/ IS tracked, only the model binary is not.
src/web_scraper/models/*.onnx

bronze_data/

silver_data/

gold_data/

.env

logs/

mt_env/

# experiments 8 and 9 each need an OCR stack that cannot share one environment (paddlepaddle vs
# onnxruntime, and both pin their own numpy), so each has its own venv beside mt_env. Both are
# rebuilt by the commands in the experiments' READMEs.
ocr_env8/
ocr_env9/

# the deepdoc_vietocr checkout + its ONNX models (~200 MB), re-fetched by
# experiment_9/setup_vendor.py
experiment/experiment_9/vendor/

# the raw OCR read, cached so the table logic can be re-run without a re-OCR
experiment/experiment_*/out/ocr_cache.json
experiment/experiment_*/out_batch/cache/

wandb/

*.npy

*.csv
!experiment_history.csv
# per-ticker feature-group -> bronze-table map
!src/data_preprocessor/feature_groups.csv
# experiment_4 disclosure-date deliverable + user-editable overrides are tracked
!experiment/experiment_4/vcb_quarter_publish_dates.csv
!experiment/experiment_4/vcb_quarter_publish_dates_detail.csv
!experiment/experiment_4/vcb_manual_overrides.csv
# experiment_5 shares-outstanding deliverable + inputs are all tracked
!experiment/experiment_5/*.csv
# experiment_6 news deliverable tracked
!experiment/experiment_6/*.csv
# experiment_7 financial-statement deliverable tracked
!experiment/experiment_7/*.csv
# experiment_4/5 raw JSON caches are regenerable (--refresh); don't track them
experiment/experiment_4/*.json
experiment/experiment_5/*.json
# experiment_7 PDF working dirs — filings are large and re-downloadable from CafeF
experiment/experiment_7/pdf_cache/
experiment/experiment_7/pdf_pages/
*.pdf
# but the progress reports at the repo root are deliverables, not filings
!/THESIS_*.pdf

*.txt
# ⚠️ ...but a dependency list is CODE, not a scraped artefact. `*.txt` swallowed
# src/kaggle_gpu/requirements.txt silently: `git add` reported success and
# committed nothing, which is the same failure this file warns about below for
# loose files under reports/.
!src/kaggle_gpu/requirements.txt

# Model run outputs (checkpoints, tensorboard, plots) — reproducible, data referenced
src/model/runs/*/
# but keep the cross-run leaderboard index
!src/model/runs/index.csv

src/train_test_set/

# The financial statements ARE the deliverable, and they are small (~0.3 MB): the four charts
# of accounts, the ticker -> template map, and the parsed statements themselves. They cost
# hours of OCR to produce, so they are tracked rather than regenerated. This negation must come
# after the blanket `*.csv` above — the last matching rule wins.
!raw_data/cafef/financials/**/*.csv
!raw_data/cafef/financials/*.csv

ocr_env8/Lib/

ocr_env9/Lib/

# Dagster instance home (run history, logs, telemetry) - regenerable
.dagster/
.tmp_dagster*/
# experiment_10 news_result.py embedding / frame cache (hundreds of MB)
experiment/experiment_10/_cache/

# Feature-selection run folders are NOT ignored — every run is committed on purpose
# (2026-08-09). The old `reports/feature_selection/*/` rule was dropped because it
# silently hid each new run until someone remembered `git add -f`, and the archive is
# the evidence behind CONTEXT §6-§14.
#
# ⚠️ Dropping that rule was NOT enough, and the 384 files already in the index hid it:
# a tracked file ignores .gitignore, so the archive looked committed while a NEW run's
# `outstanding.csv` and `feature_importance.csv` were still caught by the blanket
# `*.csv` (line 50) and any `*.txt` by line 74. These negations must come AFTER both —
# the last matching rule wins — and they re-include the whole tree, so `figures/*.png`,
# `metadata.json` and `README.md` stay covered too.
!reports/feature_selection/
!reports/feature_selection/**
#
# ⚠️ **ONE QUARANTINED ROOT, ADDED 2026-08-14, AND IT NEEDS ITS OWN PAIR — WHICH IS THE
# WHOLE ARGUMENT AGAINST EXTRA ROOTS, PAID ONCE ON PURPOSE.**
# `reports/feature_selection__forex_return_5day/` holds ONE run: `pool__forex` against
# `return_5day` at d20/h5, the first selection in this repo to measure a bar on a return
# target (`evidence=failed_null`, z = +0.12, p = 0.524).
#
# It is not in the root above because `final_features` groups by (schema, target, setup)
# with no term for which pools: it is the only `return_5day` run in the archive, so in
# the main root it would form a fresh group whose table name is
# `unified_schema_vcb.return_5day__final__d20_h5` — a table that already exists with 754
# columns — and propose rebuilding it from 44 forex channels (the STL-1 domino).
#
# ⚠️ **RETIRE THIS PAIR when either is true**: a `--scope`-named build makes the
# collision impossible, or the orphan 754-column table is dropped. Until then, without
# these two lines the run's CSVs fall straight back into the blanket `*.csv` on line 50
# and the evidence is lost the way issue RPR-1 lost 29 run folders.
!reports/feature_selection__forex_return_5day/
!reports/feature_selection__forex_return_5day/**
#
# ⚠️ A LOOSE FILE DIRECTLY UNDER `reports/` NEEDS ITS OWN NEGATION TOO — the two pairs
# above re-include SUBTREES, and neither matches a file sitting beside them. Added
# 2026-08-14 for `forex_exchanges.csv`, a generated inventory of the 48 forex exchanges
# (see `orchestration/CONTEXT.md`). Without this line the blanket `*.csv` on line 50
# takes it and `git add` reports success while committing nothing — the exact shape of
# issue GIT-1.
!reports/*.csv
#
# ⚠️ ONE ROOT, and only one, since 2026-08-10. `reports/feature_selection_basic/`,
# `_economy/` and `_superseded/` were three MORE paths these two rules did not match,
# each needing its own negation pair or its CSVs fell straight back into the blanket
# `*.csv`. They were merged into the single root above and all 22 archived runs were
# deleted, so the pair above is again the whole rule.
#
# ⚠️ What those roots bought is now bought by `--scope`, and it is NOT automatic.
# `final_features` groups every run under one root by (schema, target, setup) — a key
# with no term for "which pools" — and every entry point now seeds at 18, so a
# `pool__basic` run and a `basic+economy_<country>` run in this root are ONE group and
# are UNIONED into `return_5day__final__d20_h5`. Name the feature block per build
# (`--scope basic`, `--scope economy_japan`) or accept that union on purpose. If a run
# is ever REDONE, delete or move the superseded folder out of `reports/` — it would
# otherwise be counted as a second contribution to its replacement's table.
#
# ⚠️ They are not small. The deleted archive was ~107 MB across 22 runs, and `channel_correlation.csv`
# alone reached 40.0 MB on a 1,458-channel pool — it is the full N×N pairwise matrix.
# Before committing a new WIDE-pool run, check that one file: the deliverables are
# `feature_importance.csv` and `outstanding.csv`, and `dropped_for` already records
# what each channel was pruned into.
